@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch` already exists with private helpers (`clone`, `equal`, `isObject`, pointer parsing). RFC 6902 does
+not prescribe diffing, so the output is validated by round-trip through `applyPatch`.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Recursive structural diff.** Equal values give no ops. Differing types (including array vs object, `null`
+  vs object) give a `replace` at that path. Objects: `remove` for keys only in `from`, recurse for common keys,
+  `add` for keys only in `to`; keys are enumerated with `Object.keys`/`Object.hasOwn`, never by property lookup,
+  so `__proto__` is an ordinary key.
+- **Array alignment by longest common subsequence** (deep equality, after trimming common prefix and suffix).
+  Between matched items, the unmatched old/new runs are paired positionally and diffed recursively (so a
+  changed element yields a deep op); surplus old items yield `remove`, surplus new items yield `add`. Ops are
+  emitted in order while tracking the running index, so every index is valid when applied. A moved item shows
+  up as one `remove` plus one `add`; `move` is not emitted (allowed by the request, simpler, and always correct).
+- **Pointer escaping** `~`→`~0` first, then `/`→`~1` (RFC 6901 §3).
+- **Copies.** Values placed in `add`/`replace` are deep-cloned with the existing `clone`.
+- **No dependencies.** LCS is O(n·m) on the trimmed middle; acceptable for config/record-sized arrays.
+
+## Risks
+
+Quadratic cost for very large, very different arrays; noted, not mitigated.
