@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+## Why
+
+Clients want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document rather than the whole new
+version. The library can apply a JSON Patch (`applyPatch`) but cannot produce one.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Add `createPatch(from, to)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, returning a JSON Patch that turns `from` into `to`
+  when passed to `applyPatch`.
+- The patch is small: it descends into objects and arrays and describes only the differing locations. An array
+  item that is inserted or removed yields one `add` or `remove`; neighbours are not rewritten. An item that
+  moves within an array yields a `remove` plus an `add` (no rewrite of the items in between).
+- Neither input is mutated; values placed in the patch are copies.
+
+No breaking changes: this is a purely additive export.
+
+## Governing standard
+
+RFC 6902 (JSON Patch) §4 (operations), §4.1 `add`, §4.2 `remove`, §4.3 `replace`; RFC 6901 §3 and §5 for
+pointer syntax and escaping (`~0`, `~1`). RFC 6902 does not define how to compute a diff; the algorithm is a
+project choice, with correctness defined as `applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))` deep-equalling `to`.
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- Guaranteeing the globally shortest patch (array diff uses a longest-common-subsequence alignment, minimal in
+  inserted/removed items, not in all conceivable encodings).
+- Emitting `copy`, `move` or `test` operations.
+- Changing `applyPatch` or the merge-patch functions.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+<!-- none -->
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+- `json-patch`: adds the requirement that a patch can be generated from two documents.
+
+## Impact
+
+`src/index.ts` (new export and helpers), new tests in `test/`. No new dependencies.
