@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+## ADDED Requirements
+
+### Requirement: A patch can be created from two documents
+`createPatch(from, to)` SHALL return a JSON Patch (RFC 6902 §3) such that `applyPatch(from, patch)` deep-equals
+`to`. It SHALL NOT mutate `from` or `to`, and values in the patch SHALL NOT share structure with them. Equal
+inputs SHALL give `[]`. Every `path` SHALL be a valid RFC 6901 pointer, with `~` escaped as `~0` and `/` as `~1`
+(RFC 6901 §3, §5), including for keys such as `__proto__`.
+
+#### Scenario: Equal documents
+- **WHEN** `from` and `to` are deeply equal (including scalars, `null`, empty objects/arrays)
+- **THEN** the patch is `[]`
+
+#### Scenario: Root values differ
+- **WHEN** `from` is `1` and `to` is `{"a":1}`, or one is an array and the other an object, or `null` versus an object
+- **THEN** the patch is a single `replace` with path `""` (RFC 6902 §4.3)
+
+#### Scenario: Round trip
+- **WHEN** a patch is created for any pair of JSON values
+- **THEN** applying it to `from` yields a value deeply equal to `to`
+
+#### Scenario: Inputs are not mutated
+- **WHEN** a patch is created and later modified, or the inputs are inspected
+- **THEN** `from` and `to` are unchanged and patch values are independent copies
+
+### Requirement: The patch describes only what changed
+The patch SHALL descend into objects and arrays present in both documents and SHALL NOT replace a container
+whose type is unchanged merely because a descendant differs.
+
+#### Scenario: One small change in a large document
+- **WHEN** only `/a/b/c` differs between `from` and `to`
+- **THEN** the patch is a single operation at `/a/b/c`
+
+#### Scenario: Object members added, removed, changed
+- **WHEN** a member exists only in `to`, only in `from`, or in both with different values
+- **THEN** the patch has respectively an `add`, a `remove`, and a `replace` (or deeper operation) at that member
+
+#### Scenario: Member set to `null` or type changed
+- **WHEN** a member changes to `null`, or from an object to an array or scalar
+- **THEN** the patch is a `replace` of that member (not a removal)
+
+#### Scenario: Escaped and hostile keys
+- **WHEN** keys are `a/b`, `m~n`, `""`, `__proto__` or `constructor`
+- **THEN** paths are `/a~1b`, `/m~0n`, `/`, `/__proto__`, `/constructor`, and applying the patch gives the right result without touching prototypes
+
+### Requirement: Array changes are expressed as item operations
+For arrays present in both documents, an inserted item SHALL yield one `add` at its index, a removed item one
+`remove`, and neighbouring unchanged items SHALL NOT appear in the patch (RFC 6902 §4.1, §4.2). Indices SHALL be
+valid at the point the operation is applied, operations being applied in order.
+
+#### Scenario: Item inserted
+- **WHEN** `[1,2,3]` becomes `[1,9,2,3]`, or an item is added at the start or end
+- **THEN** the patch is a single `add` at the index of the new item
+
+#### Scenario: Item removed
+- **WHEN** `[1,2,3]` becomes `[1,3]`, or the first or last item is removed
+- **THEN** the patch is a single `remove` of that index
+
+#### Scenario: Item moved
+- **WHEN** `["a","b","c","d"]` becomes `["b","c","d","a"]` or `["a","d","b","c"]`
+- **THEN** the patch consists only of a `move`, or a `remove` and an `add`, of the moved item
+
+#### Scenario: Item changed in place
+- **WHEN** one array element differs and the array length is unchanged
+- **THEN** the patch targets only that element (descending into it if it is an object or array)
+
+#### Scenario: Several edits, duplicates, empty arrays
+- **WHEN** arrays with duplicate items, several insertions and removals, or an empty side are diffed
+- **THEN** the round-trip holds and unchanged items are not touched
